<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Guaira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Guaira is the main port of Venezuela, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast just north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, from which it is separated by the coastal mountain range. Fortified in the colonial era, it handled much of the country's trade, including cocoa exports. In 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> was arrested there and handed over to the Spanish, and in February 1814 Bolívar ordered the execution of hundreds of Spanish prisoners held in the port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-guaira.jpg" alt="La Guaira" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karla García Fernández · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Guaira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>La Guaira was founded in 1577 as an outlet for nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, which the Spanish had founded in 1567. The city has almost no land on which to expand, lying beside the Caribbean Sea. About 12 miles off the coast lies La Guaira Bank, an underwater ridge about 12 miles long and 4 miles wide, rising from depths of around 260 metres. Westerly currents meet it and bring nutrients to the surface, attracting whales, porpoises, seabirds, tuna, sharks and marlin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The port became important during the 18th century. Attacked by buccaneers and by English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and French fleets, it was turned into a fortified, walled city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> attacked it for the first time, and in March 1743 an English squadron of 19 ships under Admiral Charles Knowles failed to take it: coastal batteries led by Gabriel de Zuloaga, José de Iturriaga and Don Mateo Gual drove it off.</p><p>The Royal Gipuzkoan Company of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> held a trading monopoly over La Guaira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a> and was instrumental in developing large-scale cocoa production in the coastal valleys. In 1797 the crew of the English frigate HMS Hermione mutinied and delivered the ship to the Spanish authorities at La Guaira.</p><p>The great earthquake of 26 March 1812 badly damaged the town, and in the same year privateers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the United Kingdom fought a small naval battle off the port. In December 1822 an unusual hurricane struck, and at least 28 ships and about 100 lives were lost. In 1998 the state of La Guaira, then named Vargas, was created from parts of the former Federal District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, and the city became its capital. The town and port were badly damaged by the floods and mudslides of December 1999. The city's baseball team, the Tiburones de La Guaira, has played in the Venezuelan professional league since 1962.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Guaira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In February 1814 Bolívar ordered the execution of hundreds of Spanish prisoners at <b>La Guaira</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-republic-of-venezuela">Second Republic of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
